# __DISPLAY_NAME__

__DESCRIPTION__

## Develop

```bash
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
cupcake-plugin-dev validate
cupcake-plugin-dev docs --write
cupcake-plugin-dev package
```

`package` checks the plugin the way CUPCAKE checks an upload and writes `dist/__PLUGIN_NAME__-<version>.tar.gz`.

## Terms

Put the terms of each vocabulary in `terms/<key>.tsv` with the columns `id`, `name`, `description`,
`synonyms` (separated by `;`) and `obsolete`, or replace `load_terms` in `plugin.py` to read them from a
database or a remote service. It yields one dict per term with `id` and `name`, and optionally
`description`, `synonyms` (a list), `obsolete` and `data`. Columns use a vocabulary as ontology type
`plugin.__PLUGIN_NAME__.<key>`.

Set `"liveSearch": true` on a vocabulary to have CUPCAKE search it here instead of receiving all its terms.
`search_terms` answers those searches from `load_terms`; replace it to query your source directly.

New vocabularies are hidden from users until staff approve them on the Vocabularies page. The plugin keeps
loading terms while it waits.

## Install

On a CUPCAKE appliance, open Cupcake › Admin › Plugins and choose the package. CUPCAKE installs it,
registers it on a local port, writes its settings and starts it. Settings can be changed afterwards in
`/etc/cupcake/plugins/__PLUGIN_NAME__.env`.

Only the CUPCAKE backend calls the plugin. Every endpoint except `/health` requires the callback key the
backend sends during the startup handshake.

## Run it locally

```bash
PLUGIN_TOKEN=<token> CUPCAKE_BACKEND=http://localhost:8000 python3 plugin.py
```

Register the plugin first with `POST /api/v1/plugins/register/` as staff to get the token.

